WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.713
So after three months of the launch,
we only gathered about 200 users.

00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:07.674
However, only seven months
after we launched the product,

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.719
we see so many users saying like,
I want to pay to jump over the queue

00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:12.637
I want to pay to get a higher resolution.

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:15.432
I want to pay for more downloads,
I want to pay for more credits.

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:18.977
And by the end of 2023,
we had nearly 10 million ARR as well

00:00:19.102 --> 00:00:21.104
as 5 million user signups.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:23.523
But for the live streaming tool,
we didn't save any time.

00:00:23.565 --> 00:00:25.692
We didn't really help them
make more money.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.112
If you can't make more money
or save time for your users,

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.822
they just feel like it's a cool product.

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:34.576
It's cool from look and feel,
but not from a business perspective.

00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:38.455
A product market fit is probably something
that you see users are

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.000
Hi, I'm Young, I'm the co-founder
and the CEO at OpusClip, so we.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:47.046
OpusClip turns your long form video
footage into social, engaging short video

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:53.678
clips in just one click that saves
about over 99% of your time and resources

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.598
and expertise of social video production.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.310
We've gathered user signups,
supports and trust from over 5

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.730
million creators and businesses worldwide.

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:11.446
You can probably see that people who
create social apps are really introverts.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:16.159
They can't socialize in real life in real
person, so they have to figure out another

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:17.994
hacky way to socialize with people.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:21.623
Especially, I want to socialize
with people when I'm not sober.

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:24.334
I co-founded a social app company
that was called Sober.

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.754
So the name you can tell is that
after you drink, how you want to socialize

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:28.797
with other people, right?

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:32.217
We raised about $1.5 million back in 2013.

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:36.471
However, unluckily, that was just two
months before Snapchat was launched.

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:39.182
We shared a very similar concept
that all the friendship

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.769
and all the content will disappear
in a few seconds or in just a day.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:44.771
The company definitely failed
after a year.

00:01:44.813 --> 00:01:48.441
However, I really enjoyed being an
entrepreneur through that experience.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:52.612
I think in the early stage
when people become an entrepreneur is

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:57.450
probably driven by solving their own needs
and trying to expand that solution

00:01:57.492 --> 00:01:58.660
to more people, right?

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:02.372
I think people who want to be an
entrepreneur in the early days, by nature,

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:06.376
are people who want to impact more people,
impact the society and impact the world.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:10.046
So back in early 2015,
I went to China with a couple

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:11.548
other friends from Silicon Valley.

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:14.968
At that time, the popular trend was
to copy the successful business models

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:16.094
from the US to China,

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:19.681
and we discovered a pretty cool business
model from Toast that helped restaurants

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:23.560
speed up everything all
the way from POS to MarTech to HR tech.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:28.106
However, it didn't really turn out well
because of the completely different

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:30.108
landscape and ecosystem.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.278
But throughout the journey that we
realized, we are actually the helper

00:02:33.361 --> 00:02:36.948
for the local restaurant
and we should not only build a software,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.117
but also services whenever needed.

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:43.288
So I quickly formed a talent agency
focused on Food Review, just actually

00:02:43.371 --> 00:02:45.164
four months after TikTok was launched.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.127
I know that short video can redefine
how people market themselves, and we

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:51.421
recognize the huge opportunity out there.

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:54.507
I spent about four years
expanding that agency.

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:57.594
We had about nearly 500
creatives in the team.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:01.306
After running the agency
for about four years, we realized that all

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:04.893
the top talents left us because all
of them want to be a solopreneur.

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:09.188
It is pretty easy and intuitive
for a super creative person

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:10.315
to become a solopreneur.

00:03:10.398 --> 00:03:12.150
They want to create content
for themselves,

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:14.402
not for some local restaurants.

00:03:14.444 --> 00:03:15.737
So I thought about it.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.866
Instead of managing these creatives
directly in my team,

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:25.038
why don't I build some AI powered product
that can enable all of the creators?

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:29.125
There must be an AI solution
that can automate as much as possible

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.045
and remove the pain for creating contents.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.048
Because I was running the business,
I know how painful it is.

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:38.718
All the successful entrepreneurs
have some sort of altruism.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.637
They want to help others.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.556
It is pretty straightforward
that they can get benefit.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.058
The benefit is not commercial.

00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:50.939
It's not business, but it's more
spiritual benefit from helping others.

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:59.781
So the first product idea we came about
is AI powered live streaming tool.

00:03:59.864 --> 00:04:03.159
So that was during the pandemic
and everyone is working from home

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.203
and watching live streaming content.

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:08.081
So we founded the company
back in January 2022.

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:12.377
We spent about half a year building the
live streaming product with all sorts of

00:04:12.502 --> 00:04:18.341
cool AI features like AI generated memes,
AI populated interactions, and so on.

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:22.929
We realized that it is not solving
any real pain points.

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:27.684
So after three months of the launch,
we only gathered about 200 users.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.770
So these 200 users,
we actually spend a lot of effort.

00:04:30.812 --> 00:04:35.274
We want them to continuously use
Opus Studio to give us feedback,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.276
but none of them is willing to do so.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.531
So we paid them like $20, $30 a session
to use our product

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:43.199
so that we can gather the feedback.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:46.828
But after we stopped paying
for the stipend, none of them is

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.413
continuously using the product.

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:52.125
However, the last feature we built
for the AI powered live streaming product

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.087
was a clipping feature
because a lot of users keep asking us

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.797
that after the live streaming,

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:03.302
we want to repurpose the content
so that we can reuse for distribution.

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:07.640
So we added a feature that during your
live streaming, you can hit the button

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:09.142
and we will add a mark.

00:05:09.225 --> 00:05:11.728
And after the live streaming
content is ready,

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:14.314
we can deliver the clips for you directly.

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:18.651
And also we use some other AI
to recognize the cool moments.

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:22.613
That was the only feature
that these 200 users liked.

00:05:22.697 --> 00:05:26.159
They say all the other stuff are nonsense.
It doesn't help.

00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:28.327
How can I only use this feature?

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:32.248
So we decided to turn the feature
into a standalone product.

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:37.170
And amazingly, in just two weeks, tens of
thousands of users sign up for OpusClip

00:05:37.253 --> 00:05:40.715
And by the end of 2023,
we had nearly 10 million ARR,

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:42.884
as well as 5 million user sign ups.

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:45.762
And that was only seven months
after we launched the product.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.973
The real product market fit
comes from people who are

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:51.059
really willing to pay for it.

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:55.021
When we launched Opus Studio,
we asked the user during the interviews,

00:05:55.063 --> 00:05:59.358
like how much you want to pay for them
and they're too shy to give us an answer.

00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:04.155
They were like, I will probably think
about it or something like that, right?

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:08.034
So you can know that they don't have
any dedication or commitment

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:12.288
to pay for the product, but for open clip,
we see so many users saying like,

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:15.625
I want to pay to jump over the queue.
I want to pay to get a higher resolution.

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.669
I want to pay for more downloads,
I want to pay for more credits.

00:06:18.753 --> 00:06:23.591
We didn't have the payment setup
on day one, but so many users just came in

00:06:23.674 --> 00:06:26.302
to knock on our door and email us
and calling us even because we

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:29.055
really saved tens of hours for them.

00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:31.391
But for the live streaming tool,
we didn't save any time.

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:34.102
We didn't really help them
make more money.

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:37.522
If you can't make more money
or save time for your users,

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:39.482
they just feel like it's a cool product.

00:06:39.565 --> 00:06:43.277
It's cool from look and feel,
but not from a business perspective.

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:46.989
In the era of AI,
there are definitely way more teams

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:53.037
great at technologies, then teams
great at building a product market fit.

00:06:53.121 --> 00:06:55.623
They believe they have a really
strong technology through like

00:06:55.706 --> 00:06:59.335
a paper through academic research,
but the user really want it.

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:02.630
So I think definitely start with
what the user wants, stay with your user,

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:04.757
or even try to become a user.

00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:09.345
And then think about whether your
technology is the best solution,

00:07:09.428 --> 00:07:11.556
whether AI is the best solution.

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:16.894
So many AI products that can deliver over
100% of quality at times, but also really

00:07:16.978 --> 00:07:19.355
under deliver really disappointed users.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:22.650
So I think from a user experience
perspective, how you manage your

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:24.402
user expectations is really tricky.

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:28.156
Once AI delivers some results,
can user touch up on that?

00:07:28.239 --> 00:07:33.327
So that's why we spend a lot of our
resources building some manual features.

00:07:33.411 --> 00:07:37.665
That comes after the AI first approach,
which means once I deliver the result,

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:41.419
if the user think is 80% perfect,
they can still continue the workflow

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:43.254
from what we generate for the user.

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:47.383
This is going to really
completely solve a user problem.

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:50.761
Because you are building a product,
you are solving a business problem

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:53.723
that eventually has to be 100% resolved.

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:57.810
AI if AI can only do 80%,
you have to make sure the other 20%

00:07:57.894 --> 00:08:00.021
can be well handled by human hands.

00:08:00.104 --> 00:08:03.608
Right now at OpusClip
we launch new feature every single week.

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:06.777
That is one of our culture
and that is one of the trend.

00:08:06.861 --> 00:08:08.154
Users love our product.

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:12.408
They see opusclip not only
the current product, but how fast we out.

00:08:12.492 --> 00:08:15.661
Iterate the market by launching
new features every single week,

00:08:15.745 --> 00:08:21.042
making sure you experiment extensively
before launching an AI product.

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:26.255
Because AI is not so controllable
nowadays, the result can be versatile,

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:29.008
can go beyond even your expectation.

00:08:29.091 --> 00:08:31.719
I see most of the AI features
or AI products.

00:08:31.802 --> 00:08:36.599
Start with like a lab, start with a beta
version, and after quite a few more months

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:39.727
when it is really stable,
when the cost really came down,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.854
then it becomes part of a core product.

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:46.108
Don't spend too much time
building an AI core product.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:50.321
Try to launch it separately,
try to launch some small experiments,

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:54.116
and get early users feedback
before cutting down the cost.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.953
Before integrating that
into your main product.

00:08:57.036 --> 00:09:01.082
And our product is also very unique
in terms of user feedback

00:09:01.165 --> 00:09:05.503
is that the user not only directly give us
feedback through using the product,

00:09:05.586 --> 00:09:09.840
but also once they post a video
from our product to the social medias,

00:09:09.924 --> 00:09:13.594
we can gather the number of user views,
the number of comments, number

00:09:13.678 --> 00:09:15.638
of favorites, and number of reshares.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:20.685
So all these, what we call audience
feedback is actually ten times or even 100

00:09:20.768 --> 00:09:23.646
times more than our user direct feedback.

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:27.525
And we're going to use these amount of
feedback to fit into our model training,

00:09:27.608 --> 00:09:32.113
so that we can judge whether we
are generating a good video clip

00:09:32.196 --> 00:09:37.159
even before the user publish
to the social media channels.

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:44.500
So our vision is to enable anyone
to become a creator

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:49.213
without the effort and hassle
of video editing and prompting skills.

00:09:49.297 --> 00:09:52.133
We don't want an AI
to remove the human authenticity.

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:56.637
We want humans to focus
and spend more time on creating an idea.

00:09:56.721 --> 00:09:57.888
Create your script.

00:09:57.972 --> 00:10:03.269
Film the relevant contents and then we
OpusClip will take over all the boring

00:10:03.352 --> 00:10:05.896
and tedious and time consuming work.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.774
So there are in general,
from video production perspective,

00:10:08.858 --> 00:10:10.526
there are three major stages.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:15.031
The first one is pre-production,
where you edit a topic, you write the

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:17.491
scripts and you prepare the storyboard,

00:10:17.575 --> 00:10:21.495
and then it comes to the production
where you film the content, you record the

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:24.290
screen, or you prepare the media assets.

00:10:24.373 --> 00:10:27.793
The last one is the post-production,
right where you edit the video,

00:10:27.877 --> 00:10:32.631
you assemble, you cut the scenes
and you sequence them in order.

00:10:32.715 --> 00:10:37.636
Previously, most of the AI or technology
is only focusing on a specific scene

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:38.971
or a specific frame.

00:10:39.055 --> 00:10:42.933
But nowadays the overall story,
the overall narration and the curation

00:10:43.017 --> 00:10:47.688
can be well enhanced and automated by AI.
The second thing is definitely

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:49.357
the generative AI technologies.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.069
We can see that most of the time
it's really hard to feel the content.

00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:57.490
But nowadays with Sora, with Pika,
with other AI models, you can

00:10:57.573 --> 00:11:02.870
generate like a few seconds long footage
or B-roll from just a few words.

00:11:02.953 --> 00:11:07.583
So I think one is the understanding,
the other is the generation.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:12.129
These two huge advancement of AI is
actually automating the whole process

00:11:12.213 --> 00:11:13.881
and also generating new footage.

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:16.967
I think with the advancement
in these AI technologies,

00:11:17.051 --> 00:11:22.973
creators are actually shifting
from creating content to being creative.

00:11:23.057 --> 00:11:27.978
They don't have to spend so much time to
specifically create each piece of content.

00:11:28.062 --> 00:11:32.733
I can do this job for them, but they
should spend probably ten times more time

00:11:32.817 --> 00:11:34.318
to think about how to be creative.

00:11:34.402 --> 00:11:36.654
Because everyone is being
more and more creative.

00:11:36.737 --> 00:11:39.073
Everyone have more time
to be more creative.

00:11:39.156 --> 00:11:41.867
Everyone have more resources
to be more creative.

00:11:41.951 --> 00:11:44.829
I think the paradigm shift is
how can you be ten times tenfold

00:11:44.912 --> 00:11:46.872
more creative than before?

00:11:46.956 --> 00:11:52.920
I think in the AI era, being authentic
and being genuine is probably going to be

00:11:53.003 --> 00:11:56.674
more of a rare find because it's easy.

00:11:56.757 --> 00:12:00.886
It's super easy to leverage AI
to generate a lot of fabricated content.

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:05.307
So my advice would be,
just be yourself, be human, be real.

00:12:05.391 --> 00:12:09.228
And, you know, maybe nowadays it's
easy to be like that, but think about 5

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:14.024
or 10 years later when AI is everywhere,
when AI generated content is everywhere.

00:12:14.108 --> 00:12:20.573
Like being super real, being super genuine
and authentic is probably going

00:12:20.656 --> 00:12:23.242
to help you be the most creative.

